# Avoid Shadowing Library / API Names

Don't give a module, or an imported name, the same name as a widely-used library
or cloud API. Shadowing makes `from x import name` ambiguous and, when two
imports bind the same name, the last one silently wins — so calls resolve to the
wrong module and fail at runtime with a confusing `AttributeError`.

High-risk names to avoid for your own modules: `storage`, `client`, `bucket`,
`storage` extras (`google.cloud.storage`), `requests`, `json`, `logging`, `ee`,
`pd`/`np`, `secrets`, `types`, `io`, `queue`, `task`.

## Rules

- Suffix shared helper modules so they read as utilities, matching this repo's
  convention (`file_utils`, `gcs_utils`, `gee_utils`, `string_utils`,
  `data_storage_utils`) — not bare `storage`, `utils`, `helpers`.
- Never let two imports bind the same name in one file. If a clash is
  unavoidable, alias one explicitly (`as`).

## Example

```python
# BAD — both bind `storage`; whichever import is last wins, the other's calls break
from google.cloud import storage
from commons import storage          # shadows google.cloud.storage
storage.Client()                     # AttributeError at runtime

# GOOD — distinct module name (and a *_utils suffix)
from google.cloud import storage
from commons import data_storage_utils
storage.Client()                     # google.cloud.storage
data_storage_utils.write_csv(df, path)
```
